# The resit's Qwen3.8 reference screen (SQ8), one round, not a public row: llama.cpp mainline's fastest
# flags before W9 and W10 (docs/fair-measure.md 3). The server arms now take prose:<P> (the ids ours
# runs), each arm's own flags in its engine word; a 3090 part runs as a second call at the profile's 3090
# line (-ncmoe 43, QWEN38_NCMOE) with its fit twin as the check.
#
# Band [derived]: if op offload ran at ub 512, each of 8 ubatches of P = 4096 would copy the 26 host
# layers' experts, 26 x 1.57..1.84 GB = 40.8..47.8 GB, at 21.16 GB/s: 1.93..2.26 s a ubatch, 15.4..18.1 s,
# pp4096 <= 226..266 tok/s. The measured default (op offload on) was 356.6 (rig-log 09-28#q38-release), past
# that bound, so op offload did not run at ub 512 and -nopo 1 is the same line: 356.6 +-7 %, 330..380.
kind: baseline
question: does op offload off move llama.cpp's Qwen3.8 prompt rate at P = 4096 and ub 512, and which of
  -t 16, -nopo 1 at ub 4096 and fit does W9 carry?
term: pp tok/s @ P = 4096, llama.cpp mainline llama-server /completion prompt_per_second on the first 4096
  ids of the qwen4exp corpus-prose.ids after a same-id warm-up (-ngl 99 -fa on -lzm off -ncmoe 26 -t 32
  --no-op-offload -fit off -np 1 -ctxcp 0 --cache-ram 0, ub 512), A6000, the row `lcppsrvpp+nopo1@prose`
unit: tok/s
predict: 330..380
condition: one lease, one round, the arms lcppsrvpp:prose:4096 lcppsrvpp+nopo1:prose:4096
  lcppsrvpp4096:prose:4096 lcppsrvpp4096+nopo1:prose:4096 lcppsrv:prose:512 lcppsrv+t16:prose:512
  lcppsrvppfit:prose:4096; then with the 3090 as BLOOMERY_TIMING_GPU lcppsrv:prose:512 lcppsrvfit:prose:512;
  every server n_ctx line equal to ours' --ctx at that P; prompt_n 4096 and cache_n 0 on every pp row; no
  row [cold] past its retry, [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]; the full output to a file first. Commands:
  BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=1 BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/resit-q38-screen.card' just
  depth-gpu-qwen4exp <the A6000 arms> > <log> 2>&1, then the same with BLOOMERY_TIMING_GPU=<the 3090's
  UUID> in BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV and the 3090 arms
reason: sets the reference flags of the public Qwen3.8 windows (W9, W10), one round and unpublished;
  why now: the Qwen server arms fed lcg ids until this tree, and the 3090 line has never been loaded
decide-in: W9 runs the default line (op offload as mainline sets it) and the faster of the two ub 4096
  lines; -t 16 only if its decode row beats the plain one past 2 %
decide-below: op offload was running at ub 512 after all: -nopo 1 is llama.cpp's slower line, keep the
  default and re-read mainline's offload threshold before W9
decide-above: -nopo 1 is faster: W9 and W10 carry +nopo1 on every server arm as llama.cpp's line
